values ('d1e2f3a4-6b7c-4d61-8e01-1f2a3b4c5d11', 'anti-racist-anti-oppressive-practice', 'Anti-Racist / Anti-Oppressive Practice', 'Anti-Racist / Anti-Oppressive Practice prepares students to identify, challenge, and transform oppression in social work relationships, organizations, communities, and policy systems. Students examine racism, colonialism, classism, ableism, heterosexism, sexism, xenophobia, religious oppression, and other intersecting systems of power while developing concrete skills for ethical engagement, assessment, intervention, advocacy, organizational change, and accountability. The course is practice-centered. Students repeatedly move from theory to case analysis, field scenarios, reflective supervision, policy advocacy, organizational diagnosis, and evaluation design. The course treats self-awareness as necessary but insufficient: students must also learn how to redistribute power, change procedures, measure equity outcomes, and remain accountable to clients and communities.');

